{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ExkKcpyUsF3HVhxUX95KUY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Supabase docs Row Level Security guide\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Row Level Security\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security\"},{\"title\":\"Securing your API\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/api/securing-your-api\"},{\"title\":\"Securing your data\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/secure-data\"},{\"title\":\"RAG with Permissions\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/ai/rag-with-permissions\"},{\"title\":\"Database\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/overview\"},{\"title\":\"Tables and data\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/tables\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing: Postgres Best Practices\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/blog/postgres-best-practices-for-ai-agents\"},{\"title\":\"API keys\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/getting-started/api-keys\"},{\"title\":\"Realtime Authorization\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/realtime/authorization\"},{\"title\":\"Production Checklist\",\"url\":\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/deployment/going-into-prod\"}]\n\nThe main guide is Supabase's Row Level Security page at supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security. It's organized in three parts: how grants and policies combine, a step-by-step procedure for securing a table, and an RLS reference of helper functions and patterns. The page covers several practical points:\n\n- **Two separate checks:** Grants determine whether a role can run an operation on a table at all, and policies determine which rows that operation touches. The page says to set both for every exposed table.\n- **Default grants:** On existing projects, new public tables may already grant every privilege to anon and authenticated, and adding policies does not remove those grants.\n- **One policy per operation:** The guide recommends separate select, insert, update, and delete policies rather than a single \"for all\" policy, and it includes a worked example for a per-user profiles table.\n- **Indexes and views:** Columns that policies filter on need their own btree index. Views bypass RLS by default, and the guide shows how to use security_invoker on Postgres 15+ to change that.\n\nRelated pages worth reading alongside it are \"Securing your API\" (grants plus RLS for the Data API), \"API keys\" (which key maps to which Postgres role, and why secret keys bypass RLS), and the Production Checklist.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30423dd0-6968-468b-8e6b-480cbba64ffe","uuid":"4980314f-c75a-4693-8830-005c8c193eb9","timestamp":"2026-10-16T23:09:02.702Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Supabase docs Row Level Security guide","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01T497CL5nWrwHBkg49ELgU4","content":[{"title":"Row Level Security","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security"},{"title":"Securing your API","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/api/securing-your-api"},{"title":"Securing your data","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/secure-data"},{"title":"RAG with Permissions","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/ai/rag-with-permissions"},{"title":"Database","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/overview"},{"title":"Tables and data","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/tables"},{"title":"Introducing: Postgres Best Practices","url":"https://supabase.com/blog/postgres-best-practices-for-ai-agents"},{"title":"API keys","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/getting-started/api-keys"},{"title":"Realtime Authorization","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/realtime/authorization"},{"title":"Production Checklist","url":"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/deployment/going-into-prod"}]},"The main guide is Supabase's Row Level Security page at supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security. It's organized in three parts: how grants and policies combine, a step-by-step procedure for securing a table, and an RLS reference of helper functions and patterns. The page covers several practical points:\n\n- **Two separate checks:** Grants determine whether a role can run an operation on a table at all, and policies determine which rows that operation touches. The page says to set both for every exposed table.\n- **Default grants:** On existing projects, new public tables may already grant every privilege to anon and authenticated, and adding policies does not remove those grants.\n- **One policy per operation:** The guide recommends separate select, insert, update, and delete policies rather than a single \"for all\" policy, and it includes a worked example for a per-user profiles table.\n- **Indexes and views:** Columns that policies filter on need their own btree index. Views bypass RLS by default, and the guide shows how to use security_invoker on Postgres 15+ to change that.\n\nRelated pages worth reading alongside it are \"Securing your API\" (grants plus RLS for the Data API), \"API keys\" (which key maps to which Postgres role, and why secret keys bypass RLS), and the Production Checklist."],"durationSeconds":5.868109413,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ExkKcpyUsF3HVhxUX95KUY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
